=== AUDIT — CV1-LIFECYCLE-UX-001 / task 1.9 ===
DATE: 2026-05-02T10:04+02:00
AUDIT_CHANNEL: cursor-session
AUDIT_FALLBACK_REASON: claude-terminal Anthropic Pro quota
AUDIT_VERDICT: ESCALATED
ESCALATION_TYPE: soft_gate_plan_drift (per human-gates.mdc — Claude audit detects plan drift)
ROUND: 1 / 5 (no rework loop applicable — architectural decision required)
FINDINGS:
  Codex (codex-extension, gpt-5.5-pro xhigh) correctly halted before any product edit per declared halt-condition. The plan §1.9 contract requires `lockForUpdate()` to operate "inside the existing transaction context", but the actual runtime call path is:
    OrderService::create / FrontendOrderService::create -> DB::transaction commit
    -> OrderCreated event (DispatchableAfterCommit, hardened in task 1.6)
    -> EventServiceProvider maps OrderCreated to DecrementItemAvailabilityOnOrder
    -> Listener calls AvailabilityService::decrementForOrder($order) AFTER commit (no surrounding tx)
  Adding lockForUpdate in this context is meaningless (no transaction to scope the lock).
  Wrapping in a new local transaction was explicitly forbidden by the brief because it would change lifecycle semantics (when ItemAvailabilityChanged fires).
  Codex evidence cross-checked by Claude orchestrator (in-session): listener file confirmed at app/Listeners/DecrementItemAvailabilityOnOrder.php, OrderCreated trait confirmed DispatchableAfterCommit (just hardened in task 1.6).
SCOPE_RESPECTED: yes (Codex modified only reports/handoff/blocks/NEEDS_CLAUDE_M2_1_9.md, post_execute_latest.log, AGENT_ACTIVITY_LOG.md — no product file touched).
ARCHITECTURAL_OPTIONS_FOR_HUMAN:
  Option 1 — Move AvailabilityService::decrementForOrder INTO OrderService transaction (alongside StockService::decrementForOrder), remove the after-commit listener path. Pros: lockForUpdate becomes meaningful, atomic with stock decrement. Cons: changes ItemAvailabilityChanged dispatch timing (now during order tx — but the event itself is also DispatchableAfterCommit so net timing OK); requires careful symmetry review (FoodKing invariant #5).
  Option 2 — Authorize a local DB::transaction inside AvailabilityService::decrementForOrder + lockForUpdate inside it. Pros: smallest diff, listener path preserved. Cons: subdivides the lifecycle (event timing inside listener slightly different from current); introduces nested transaction-like behavior.
  Option 3 — Pivot strategy from "lockForUpdate" to ATOMIC SQL increment (DB::table('item_branch_availability')->where(...)->update(['daily_consumed_qty' => DB::raw('LEAST(daily_consumed_qty + ?, max_daily_qty)'), ...])). Pros: lock-free, race-free at row level, no transaction needed, cleanest. Cons: requires more careful event-flip detection (UPDATE returns affected rows; need a second query to read the new state for the is_available flip decision); replaces plan's prescribed implementation strategy.
RECOMMENDED_BY_CLAUDE_ORCHESTRATOR: Option 3 (atomic UPDATE) — sidesteps the entire transaction-boundary question, race-free without a lock, and aligns with the spirit of §1.9 ("garantir que 2 commandes simultanées ... ne perdent pas un increment"). Plan §1.9 would need a small amendment.
